{"name":"Gin Berry","hex":"#a3b9b6","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"gin berry","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["a plant of the species Glycosmis pentaphylla"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gin_berry"}],"entries":[{"title":"Gin","blocks":[{"text":"Gin is a distilled alcoholic drink flavoured with juniper berries and other botanical ingredients. It originated as a medicinal liquor made by monks and alchemists in the 12th Century in Salerno, Italy. The modern gin was modified in Flanders and the Netherlands to provide aqua vita from distillates of grapes and grains, becoming an object of commerce in the spirits industry. Gin became popular in England after the introduction of jenever, a Dutch and Belgian liquor. Although this development had been taking place since the early 17th century, gin became widespread after the 1688 Glorious Revolution led by William of Orange and subsequent import restrictions on French brandy. Gin emerged as the national alcoholic drink of England during the Gin Craze of 1695–1735."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/DecaturGins.jpg/330px-DecaturGins.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gin","wikidata":"Q959362"},{"title":"Berry","blocks":[{"text":"A berry is a small, pulpy, and often edible fruit. Typically, berries are juicy, rounded, brightly colored, sweet, sour or tart, and do not have a stone or pit although many pips or seeds may be present.‍ Common examples of berries in the culinary sense are strawberries, raspberries, blueberries, blackberries, white currants, blackcurrants, and redcurrants.‍ In Britain, soft fruit is a horticultural term for such fruits.‍"}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Berberis_thunbergii_berries.jpg/330px-Berberis_thunbergii_berries.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berry","wikidata":"Q16128920"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/William_Hogarth_-_Gin_Lane.jpg/500px-William_Hogarth_-_Gin_Lane.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/William_Hogarth_-_Gin_Lane.jpg/1280px-William_Hogarth_-_Gin_Lane.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/William_Hogarth_-_Gin_Lane.jpg/500px-William_Hogarth_-_Gin_Lane.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hogarth's Gin Lane (created 1750–1751)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWilliam_Hogarth_-_Gin_Lane.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/The_Gin_shop_-_Cruikshank%2C_Scraps_and_sketches_%281829%29%2C_f.9_-_BL.jpg/500px-The_Gin_shop_-_Cruikshank%2C_Scraps_and_sketches_%281829%29%2C_f.9_-_BL.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/The_Gin_shop_-_Cruikshank%2C_Scraps_and_sketches_%281829%29%2C_f.9_-_BL.jpg/1280px-The_Gin_shop_-_Cruikshank%2C_Scraps_and_sketches_%281829%29%2C_f.9_-_BL.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/The_Gin_shop_-_Cruikshank%2C_Scraps_and_sketches_%281829%29%2C_f.9_-_BL.jpg/500px-The_Gin_shop_-_Cruikshank%2C_Scraps_and_sketches_%281829%29%2C_f.9_-_BL.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"George Cruikshank's engraving of The Gin Shop (1829)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_Gin_shop_-_Cruikshank%2C_Scraps_and_sketches_(1829)%2C_f.9_-_BL.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Bee%27s_Knees.jpg/500px-Bee%27s_Knees.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedi